---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
import { componentsSidebar } from '../../data/nav';
import withHintHtml from '../../examples/input/with-hint.html?raw';
import withErrorHtml from '../../examples/input/with-error.html?raw';
import disabledHtml from '../../examples/input/disabled.html?raw';

const onThisPage = [
  { label: 'Preview', href: '#preview' },
  { label: 'When to use', href: '#when-to-use' },
  { label: 'When not to use', href: '#when-not-to-use' },
  { label: 'Labels and hint text', href: '#labels-and-hint-text' },
  { label: 'Choosing the right type', href: '#choosing-the-right-type' },
  { label: 'Errors', href: '#errors' },
  { label: 'Disabled inputs', href: '#disabled-inputs' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="Input"
  section="components"
  description="Use the input component to let users enter a single line of free-form text."
  pageTitle="Input"
  pageSubtitle="Lets users enter a single line of short, free-form text."
  sidebarGroups={componentsSidebar}
  sidebarLabel="Components"
  onThisPage={onThisPage}
>
  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <h2 id="preview">Preview</h2>
  </div>
  <Example title="Input with hint text" html={withHintHtml.trim()} />

  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <p>
      The Input component lets users enter a single line of short, free-form text, such
      as a name, reference number, or email address. Every input is paired with a
      visible label so it is clear what answer is expected.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this component</h2>
    <p>
      Use an input for short, single-line answers where you cannot offer a fixed set of
      choices, such as a name, address line, or reference number. Choose a specific
      <code>type</code> — for example <code>email</code>, <code>tel</code>, or
      <code>url</code> — so mobile keyboards and browser validation match the answer
      you're asking for.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-not-to-use">When not to use this component</h2>
    <p>
      Do not use an input when the answer spans more than a sentence — use a text area
      instead. Do not use an input for numeric answers that need step controls, such as
      a quantity; use a number input instead. And do not use an input when you can offer
      users a fixed set of choices — a select, radio buttons, or checkboxes are easier
      and less error-prone to answer.
    </p>

    <h2 id="labels-and-hint-text">Labels and hint text</h2>
    <p>
      Always pair an input with a visible <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> — never rely on
      placeholder text as a label, because placeholder text disappears as soon as the
      user starts typing and is easy to miss altogether. Use hint text (<code
        >.govbb-hint</code
      >) beneath the label to explain a format or give an example, such as "As it
      appears on your ID", and link it to the input with <code>aria-describedby</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="choosing-the-right-type">Choosing the right type</h2>
    <p>
      Pick the <code>type</code> attribute that matches the expected answer —
      <code>email</code>, <code>tel</code>, <code>url</code>, or <code>password</code> —
      so mobile keyboards and built-in validation behave correctly. Avoid
      <code>type="number"</code> on a plain input; use the dedicated number input
      component instead when you need step controls.
    </p>

    <h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
    <p>
      When an input fails validation, show an error message (<code
        >.govbb-error-message</code
      >) above the input with <code>role="alert"</code>, and mark the input itself with
      <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> and <code>aria-describedby</code> pointing at the
      error message id. Keep error messages specific and actionable, such as "Enter a
      valid email address".
    </p>
  </div>
  <Example title="Input with error" html={withErrorHtml.trim()} />

  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <h2 id="disabled-inputs">Disabled inputs</h2>
    <p>
      Avoid disabling inputs wherever possible. A disabled input gives the user no
      information about what they need to do to enable it, and its content is skipped
      by many screen readers. Prefer showing the value as read-only text, or explaining
      why the field is unavailable.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Example title="Disabled input" html={disabledHtml.trim()} />
</ArticleLayout>
